//
// A tuple comparison must not invent an ordering its ELEMENTS do not have.
//
// `<` lowers to the element types' own operators, so a tuple of booleans is exactly as
// orderable as a bare boolean — which in loft is not at all. The diagnostic names the
// ELEMENT (`'boolean' and 'boolean'`), not the tuple around it, so it points at the thing
// the author has to change. Equality is unaffected: booleans have `==`, and
// `tuple-comparisons.loft` asserts a boolean element compares with it.
//
// Its own file: the error fires in pass 2, and an earlier error in a shared file would
// abort the pass before this line is reached.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: No matching operator '<' on 'boolean' and 'boolean'
fn tuple_of_booleans_has_no_ordering() {
  println("{(false, 1) < (true, 0)}");
}
